;;; `open` written as a premise pattern's argument loads, as a name of the
;;; program — regression `premise-value`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `r`'s premise `(p open)` asks for the fact `p` holds of `open`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `open` is a name wherever the kernel does not read it, at the head of a
;;; written fact, a premise or a conclusion, and under an ordinary relation the
;;; head of a fact the kernel does not read (Q-M1f.66, Q-M1f.571): here it is
;;; written as a premise pattern's argument, so the program loads, and every
;;; command answers.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `f62e34e1`, release: the program loads, `open` is a name of the program,
;;; so `(p open)` is a candidate and `r` fires under it: R is 22 states, exit 0
;;; on every command.
;;; The engine loads it.
;;;

(relation a Thing :why "{?3} is an a")
(relation p Thing :why "{?3} is a p")
(relation q Thing :why "{?3} is a q")
(is-a k Thing)
(a k)
(rule r ()
  :match  (and (a ?x) (p open))
  :assert (q ?x)
  :why "{?x} is an a, and open is a p, so {?x} is a q")
